{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.InvariantFormTransport"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.gauge_ratio_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.gauge_ratio_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GaugeGroupThreeScale.lean#L130-L145","doc":"**Gauge invariance of Schur-scalar ratios (route C I1, over `ℝ`).**\nTwo Schur scalars `c₁, c₂` transported under the *same* three-scale gauge\naction pick up the same factor `λ/(μ·s²)`, so their ratio is unchanged:\nthe ratio is a degree-0 (gauge-invariant) observable. This is the Lean\nfooting for the probe's route-C I1 reading that only ratio-spectrum shapes\nare intrinsic — magnitudes of individual scalars are convention-carrying.\n(For an admissible gauge — `λ, μ, s` all nonzero — the common factor is\nnonzero and cancels; no `c₂ ≠ 0` hypothesis is needed, since with Mathlib's\ndivision convention the identity also holds degenerately. The probe's\nroute-C instance has `c₂ ≠ 0`.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.three_scale_gauge_law_probe_orientation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.three_scale_gauge_law_probe_orientation","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GaugeGroupThreeScale.lean#L106-L126","doc":"**The composite three-scale gauge law in the probe's orientation\n(K1, literal form).** The probe defines `c` with the intertwiner on the\nHodge side, `G_M = c • (Pᵀ * G_H * P)`; there the gauge action\n`(G_M, G_H, P) ↦ (λ • G_M, μ • G_H, s • P)` transforms the scalar as\n`c ↦ (λ/(μ·s²))·c` — the exact factor asserted (and machine-verified to\n5e-60) by `K1_three_scale_gauge_group` in\n`alpha_em_b2_canonical_scale_ratio_probe.py`. The two orientations are the\nsame gauge law: the `s`-exponent flips sign with the side of the pullback. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.three_scale_gauge_law","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.three_scale_gauge_law","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GaugeGroupThreeScale.lean#L84-L104","doc":"**The composite three-scale gauge law (K1, transported-form\norientation).** Combining the Markov leg, the Hodge leg, and the proved\nintertwiner covariance `invariantForm_pullback_smul`: under the full gauge\naction `(M, G, P) ↦ (λ • M, μ • G, s • P)` with `μ ≠ 0`,\n\n    (s • P)ᵀ * (λ • M) * (s • P) = (λ * s² / μ * c) • (μ • G),\n\ni.e. the Schur scalar transforms as `c ↦ (λ·s²/μ)·c`. This is the audit's\nK1 law: the gauge group has THREE scales, so two canonical Gram pins still\nleave the intertwiner gauge `s²` — a fully normalization-free magnitude is\nimpossible with two pins. The gauge law constrains conventions only; it\ndoes not fix the τ-scale (that third scale is the open τ-scale gate). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.invariantForm_hodge_rescale","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.invariantForm_hodge_rescale","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GaugeGroupThreeScale.lean#L73-L82","doc":"**Hodge leg of the three-scale gauge law.** If `Pᵀ * M * P = c • G` and\n`μ ≠ 0`, then relative to the rescaled Hodge Gram `μ • G` the same\ntransported form has Schur scalar `c / μ`:\n`Pᵀ * M * P = (c / μ) • (μ • G)`. Rescaling the Hodge side rescales `c` by\n`μ⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.invariantForm_markov_rescale","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.GaugeGroupThreeScale.invariantForm_markov_rescale","kind":"theorem","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GaugeGroupThreeScale.lean#L57-L65","doc":"**Markov leg of the three-scale gauge law.** If the transported Markov\nGram is proportional to the Hodge Gram, `Pᵀ * M * P = c • G`, then rescaling\nthe Markov Gram `M ↦ λ • M` rescales the Schur scalar by `λ`:\n`Pᵀ * (λ • M) * P = (λ * c) • G`. Purely algebraic — stated over a generic\ncommutative ring per the base-ring convention (§7c). ","signatureChunk":0}]}